  "body": "The new `document.open` model still involves firing a `load` event sometime after `document.close`, via the \"the end\" bits in the spec for the parser.\r\n\r\nThis means that you can get multiple `load` events fired for the same document.  How does this interact with `PerformanceNavigationTiming`'s `loadEventStart` and `loadEventEnd` attributes?  With the old model, I think, the document got a new `PerformanceNavigationTiming` on `open()`, so it just ended up with the values from the new firing and it all kinda made sense.  In the new model, what happens and why?\r\n\r\nThe navigation timing spec has language like:\r\n\r\n> equal to the time immediately before the load event of the current document is fired.\r\n\r\nwhich assumes (note the \"the\") that there is only one `load` event.  Which just isn't the case.\r\n\r\n@igrigorik @domenic @TimothyGu ",
